1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
<v Interviewer>Thanks for making time. Can you take me through discharging a patient from the ward?</v>

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:18.000
<v Helen>Of course. What we're after is getting someone home safely, with the right medicines and support, and freeing the bed for the next person.</v>

3
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:26.000
<v Helen>I'm Helen, I'm a staff nurse on the ward. Ravi's one of our doctors, he'll jump in.</v>

4
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:31.000
<v Ravi>Hi. Yeah, I'll chip in on the medical side.</v>

5
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:43.000
<v Helen>The bit everyone notices is the end, when I tell the bed manager the bed's free. They don't trust the system, so I tell them myself.</v>

6
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:53.000
<v Helen>But let me start at the beginning. It starts on the ward round. The consultant or Ravi decides the patient is medically fit to go.</v>

7
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:59.000
<v Ravi>Then one of us writes the discharge summary on the system.</v>

8
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:03.000
<v Interviewer>How often do you do a discharge?</v>

9
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:08.000
<v Helen>Every day. Several a shift, usually.</v>

10
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:16.000
<v Helen>Then I sit down with the patient and go through the discharge letter and every medicine.</v>

11
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:28.000
<v Helen>Sorry, no, that's later. First it's the medicines. If they've changed, the doctor writes the TTO, that's the to-take-out prescription.</v>

12
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:35.000
<v Helen>If nothing's changed, we just give them back their own medicines from the locker.</v>

13
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:44.000
<v Helen>The TTO goes to pharmacy and they dispense it. That can take two to four hours.</v>

14
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:53.000
<v Helen>Honestly, waiting for TTOs is the biggest hold-up. It happens most days, it drives me mad.</v>

15
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:58.000
<v Helen>When it's late, I phone pharmacy to chase it.</v>

16
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:06.000
<v Helen>When the medicines arrive, I check them against the discharge summary, every item.</v>

17
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:18.000
<v Helen>Then transport. If they need hospital transport, I book it on the transport portal, and transport confirm a pickup slot, usually by email.</v>

18
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:23.000
<v Helen>If family are collecting, I don't need to book anything.</v>

19
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:29.000
<v Interviewer>Going back to pharmacy, what if they can't get the TTO done in time?</v>

20
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:47.000
<v Helen>Oh, right. If they say it'll be ready within two hours, I carry on and check it when it comes up. If not, I walk down and collect it myself. I've done that loads of times, sometimes twice in a shift. Then I'm back to checking the medicines.</v>

21
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:58.000
<v Ravi>Can I add, on the summary: when I can, I decide the evening before and write it then, so it's ready first thing.</v>

22
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:03.000
<v Interviewer>How hard is the summary to write, on a scale of one to five?</v>

23
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:09.000
<v Ravi>A three. It's not hard, it's just time I don't have.</v>

24
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:20.000
<v Helen>Then, do they need a care package at home? If yes, I refer them to the community care team. That can add a day, easily.</v>

25
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:26.000
<v Helen>Once they've agreed, I confirm the care start date with the patient.</v>

26
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:34.000
<v Helen>With the family, I mean, not the patient. The family sort out who's there on the first day.</v>

27
00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:38.000
<v Helen>If they don't need care, I skip all that.</v>

28
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:48.000
<v Helen>Then the teaching: going through the letter and the medicines, like I said. The patient asks questions and I get them to tell it back to me.</v>

29
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:58.000
<v Helen>Oh, before that, I take out their cannula. Um, I always do that before the teaching, so they're not distracted by it.</v>

30
00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:06.000
<v Ravi>I sometimes do the medicines talk myself if it's complicated, like a new anticoagulant.</v>

31
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:17.000
<v Helen>I give them a copy of the discharge letter. Um, and the GP gets it, er, electronically I think, the system sends it.</v>

32
00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:25.000
<v Helen>Then, if transport's booked, the crew collect them from the ward. If not, family pick them up.</v>

33
00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:31.000
<v Helen>Transport not turning up, that happens maybe once a week.</v>

34
00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:43.000
<v Helen>When it does, I ring the transport desk. I've got all their numbers on a sticky note on the station, the portal's useless for that.</v>

35
00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:51.000
<v Helen>Sometimes they can rebook for later that day and we carry on waiting for the crew.</v>

36
00:04:51.000 --> 00:05:03.000
<v Helen>If they can't, I have to tell the patient they're staying another night. That happens a couple of times a month, and it breaks my heart every time.</v>

37
00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:11.000
<v Helen>Once they've gone, I update the bed status on the system, and then I tell the bed manager, like I said.</v>

38
00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:15.000
<v Helen>And that's them discharged home.</v>

39
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:21.000
<v Interviewer>On a scale of one to five, how hard is the whole discharge for you?</v>

40
00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:25.000
<v Helen>A four. On a bad day a five.</v>

41
00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:30.000
<v Interviewer>And how long from medically fit to out the door?</v>

42
00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:35.000
<v Helen>Three or four hours on a good day.</v>

43
00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:40.000
<v Helen>No, be honest, Helen. Four or five hours on a good day.</v>

44
00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:48.000
<v Ravi>From my side it's more like half an hour of actual work, spread across the day.</v>

